From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Morinaga <me@gustavomorinaga.dev>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 10:31:06 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] cellPad: several virtual pads, hot-plug by command, scripted
 input per port

Test tools for local two-player games, which need two controllers and a way
to drive the second port without a person at it. All of it is read from
environment variables that nothing sets in a release.

- PAD_VIRTUAL takes a comma list (PAD_VIRTUAL=xbox,ps5): one SDL virtual
  controller per entry, in that order. Pad 0 reads its state from
  PAD_VIRTUAL_FILE as before, pad n from PAD_VIRTUAL_FILE_n. Each pad keeps
  its own joystick handle; before, the feeder opened the first virtual
  joystick it found, so a second pad could never be driven.
- A command file that says "detach" unplugs its pad
  (SDL_JoystickDetachVirtual) and "attach" plugs it in again. The pad comes
  back as a new instance with the same GUID, so the hot-plug code puts it
  back into the port it had: a controller switched off and on mid-game,
  without hardware.
- PAD_FILE_n and PAD_SCRIPT_n (n = 1..3) drive ports 1 to 3 the way
  PAD_FILE and PAD_SCRIPT drive port 0. A port takes them only while it
  reads connected.
- PAD_VIRTUAL_KEYS=n: the keyboard, by the key map, drives virtual pad n
  instead of port 0, so one person with one real pad can test pad + pad by
  hand. The mouse stays on port 0.

The text parsing (the list, the variable names, the command file) is in
pad_test_input.h, which the host test tools/tests/test_pad_test_input.sh
runs without SDL.
